  "mechanisms": [
    "Aesthetic experiences in the built environment are mediated by three large-scale neural systems: knowledge-meaning (cognition), emotion-valuation (emotion), and sensorimotor (behavior).",
    "Coherence is primarily driven by the perception of organization and relates to fluency theory, involving cognitive, affective, and behavioral responses.",
    "Hominess relates to the Danish concept of 'hygge' (coziness, warmth, togetherness), depending on interactions between sensory inputs (naturalistic stimuli) and affective processing (belonging).",
    "Fascination is a vector sum of complexity and interest, akin to Kaplan's exploration dimension, where informational richness triggers interest and motivates exploration.",
    "The visual brain harbors sensitivities to psychological dimensions of coherence, fascination, and hominess, with valuation varying by dimension and task.",
    "Neural signatures represent top-down influences on visual valuation rather than bottom-up psychophysical properties of the visual images."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Task (beauty judgment vs. approach-avoidance decisions)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "Architects can test the psychological impact of proposed design schemes (pre-construction) along the dimensions of coherence, fascination, and hominess.",
    "The identified components can be used for post-occupancy evaluations of buildings.",
    "Architects can weigh the importance of these components differently based on the building type (e.g., home, hospital, library, museum)."
  ],
